{"explanation":"For f(x) = (2x + 1)/(x − 3), the denominator is 0 at x = 3, and the numerator isn't (it equals 7 there). That gives a vertical asymptote at x = 3, where the graph shoots up to +∞ on the right and down to −∞ on the left. The top and bottom have the same degree, so the ratio of their leading coefficients, 2/1, gives a horizontal asymptote at y = 2: for very large |x| the function behaves like 2x/x = 2. Use the slider to move the point along the curve. Near x = 3 the values blow up, and far out to either side they settle toward 2.","manimCode":"const f = (x) => (2 * x + 1) / (x - 3);

const ax = new Axes({
  xRange: [-6, 12, 2],
  yRange: [-8, 12, 2],
  xLength: 10,
  yLength: 6,
  tips: false
});
scene.add(ax);

// Asymptotes
const vAsym = new Line({ start: ax.c2p(3, -8), end: ax.c2p(3, 12), color: RED });
const hAsym = new Line({ start: ax.c2p(-6, 2), end: ax.c2p(12, 2), color: GREEN });
vAsym.setStrokeWidth(2);
hAsym.setStrokeWidth(2);

// Two branches (avoid the discontinuity, clip to y-range)
const left = ax.plot(f, { color: BLUE, xRange: [-6, 2.3], numSamples: 400 });
const right = ax.plot(f, { color: BLUE, xRange: [3.7, 12], numSamples: 400 });

const title = new MathTex({ latex: 'f(x)=\\frac{2x+1}{x-3}', color: WHITE });
await title.waitForRender();
title.scale(0.8);
